    "rawMarkdown": "# \ud83d\udc33 Day 02: Container Introductions & Docker Architecture\n\n> **\ud83d\udccc Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)  \n> **\u23f1\ufe0f Duration:** 2 Hours 37 Minutes  \n> **\ud83c\udfaf Objective:** Demystify what a container is at the OS level (Linux Namespaces & cgroups), understand Docker Engine architecture (dockerd, containerd, runc), and master container lifecycle commands.\n\n---\n\n## 1. What Exactly is a Container? (No Magic, Just Linux!)\n\nA container is NOT a mini virtual machine. A container is simply a standard Linux Process bounded by two fundamental Linux kernel features:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    subgraph \"Linux Host Kernel\"\n        subgraph \"Container Process\"\n            P1[\"Application Process (PID 1)\"]\n            NS[\"1. Linux Namespaces<br/>(Isolation: What it can see)\"]\n            CG[\"2. Control Groups - cgroups<br/>(Resource Limits: What it can consume)\"]\n            P1 --- NS\n            P1 --- CG\n        end\n    end\n```\n\n### \ud83d\udca1 Real-World Analogy:\n* **Linux Namespaces:** Like horse blinkers. The horse only sees the lane ahead and is completely oblivious to the rest of the bustling city. Similarly, namespaces restrict the process to seeing only its own resources.\n* **Control Groups (cgroups):** Like a strict ration card / budget. Regardless of appetite, you are capped at 2 CPU cores and 4GB RAM. Cgroups enforce resource quotas and prevent a rogue container from starving the host.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The 6 Core Linux Namespaces\n\n| Namespace | What It Isolates | Practical Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **PID** | Process IDs | The containerized process becomes PID 1 inside its own isolated tree. |\n| **NET** | Network Stack | Private virtual network interface (`eth0`), IP, and routing table. |\n| **MNT** | Mount Points | The container only sees its own isolated root filesystem (`/`). |\n| **UTS** | Hostname & Domain | Container has its own distinct hostname independent of host. |\n| **IPC** | Inter-Process Comm | Prevents shared memory access between different containers. |\n| **USER** | User & Group IDs | Can run as UID 0 (root) inside while mapped to an unprivileged UID outside. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Docker Engine Architecture (Under the Hood)\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    CLI[\"1. Docker Client<br/>(docker CLI)\"] -->|REST API over Socket| D[\"2. Docker Daemon<br/>(dockerd)\"]\n    D -->|gRPC| CD[\"3. Containerd<br/>(Core Lifecycle Runtime)\"]\n    CD -->|Executes| R[\"4. runc<br/>(Low-level OCI Tool)\"]\n    R -->|Configures NS & Cgroups| K[\"Linux Kernel\"]\n```\n\n1. **Docker Client:** The user-facing CLI binary that parses commands and communicates with the daemon via `/var/run/docker.sock`.\n2. **Docker Daemon (`dockerd`):** High-level service managing images, volumes, networks, and build operations.\n3. **Containerd:** Industry-standard core container runtime managing container lifecycles, image pulls, and execution.\n4. **Runc:** Lightweight CLI tool that invokes kernel system calls (`clone`, `setns`, `unshare`), creates namespaces/cgroups, and launches the process.\n5. **Containerd-Shim:** A tiny daemonless process per container that keeps the container alive even if dockerd or containerd restarts.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Golden Rule: Why Do Containers Exit? (PID 1 Lifecycle)\n\n> \u26a0\ufe0f **Golden Rule:** A container lives only as long as its primary foreground process (PID 1) is running. If PID 1 terminates, the kernel terminates the entire namespace, and the container exits!\n\n* **Why `docker run ubuntu` exits immediately:** Ubuntu's default command is `/bin/bash`. Without an interactive terminal attached, bash reaches EOF and terminates. To keep a container alive, it must run a continuous foreground process (e.g., `nginx -g \"daemon off;\"`).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Essential Docker Commands Cheat Sheet\n\n```bash\n# 1. Run a container in detached mode with port forwarding\ndocker run -d --name web-server -p 8080:80 nginx\n\n# 2. List running containers\ndocker ps\n\n# 3. List all containers (including exited)\ndocker ps -a\n\n# 4. Open an interactive bash shell inside a running container\ndocker exec -it web-server /bin/bash\n\n# 5. Stream container logs in real-time\ndocker logs -f web-server\n\n# 6. Inspect container low-level JSON details (IP, Mounts, State)\ndocker inspect web-server\n\n# 7. Graceful stop and removal\ndocker stop web-server && docker rm web-server\n```\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udfaf Day 2 Review & Interview Questions\n\n1. **What is the difference between Namespaces and Cgroups?**  \n   \ud83d\udc49 Namespaces provide isolation (what the container sees); Cgroups provide resource governance (how much CPU/RAM/IO it can use).\n2. **What is the purpose of containerd-shim?**  \n   \ud83d\udc49 A lightweight process created by containerd that acts as a parent to the container, enabling daemonless containers (Docker daemon can restart without terminating running containers).\n3. **Why does a container stop when its main process finishes?**  \n   \ud83d\udc49 Because a container's lifecycle is bound to PID 1. If PID 1 finishes its execution or exits, the container exits.\n4. **What do the `-d` and `-p` flags do in `docker run`?**  \n   \ud83d\udc49 `-d` runs the container in detached (background) mode; `-p` maps a host port to a container port (e.g., `host:container`).\n",
    "mermaid": "graph TD\n    subgraph \"Linux Host Kernel\"\n        subgraph \"Container Process\"\n            P1[\"Application Process (PID 1)\"]\n            NS[\"1. Linux Namespaces<br/>(Isolation: What it can see)\"]\n            CG[\"2. Control Groups - cgroups<br/>(Resource Limits: What it can consume)\"]\n            P1 --- NS\n            P1 --- CG\n        end\n    end",
