    "rawMarkdown": "# Day 03: AWS Lab Setup & Production Docker Installation\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Course & Topic Overview\n- **Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)\n- **Module:** Environment Provisioning & Docker Engine Setup\n- **Target Platform:** AWS EC2 (Ubuntu 22.04 / 24.04 LTS)\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why Cloud-Based Lab Setup? (AWS EC2)\n\nRunning multi-node container environments and Kubernetes clusters locally demands high CPU and RAM, often leading to host thermal throttling, kernel version mismatches, and network bridge conflicts. Using AWS EC2 gives us reproducible, isolated, and scalable cloud compute:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    Client([Local Terminal / Mac]) -->|SSH via Port 22 + .pem Key| SG[AWS Security Group]\n    subgraph \"AWS Cloud (VPC)\"\n        SG --> EC2[\"EC2 Instance: Ubuntu 22.04 LTS<br/>Instance Type: t2.medium / t3.medium (2 vCPU, 4GB RAM)\"]\n        EC2 --> Storage[\"Storage: 30GB gp3 EBS Volume\"]\n        EC2 --> Docker[\"Docker Engine (dockerd + containerd)\"]\n        EC2 --> K8sReady[\"Kubernetes Ready (Swap Disabled)\"]\n    end\n```\n\n### Recommended AWS Instance Specifications:\n- **Instance Type:** `t2.medium` or `t3.medium` (Minimum 2 vCPUs and 4GB RAM required for future Kubernetes control-plane components).\n- **AMI (Operating System):** `Ubuntu Server 22.04 LTS` or `24.04 LTS` (64-bit x86).\n- **Storage:** `30 GB General Purpose SSD (gp3)` to prevent disk exhaustion during image pulls and container builds.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Security Group Port Matrix\n\n| Port / Range | Protocol | Source | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **22** | TCP | My IP (or 0.0.0.0/0) | SSH remote access from Mac/Linux terminal. |\n| **80 / 443** | TCP | 0.0.0.0/0 | Standard HTTP/HTTPS web application traffic. |\n| **8080 - 8090** | TCP | 0.0.0.0/0 | Application container port testing & webhooks. |\n| **6443** | TCP | VPC CIDR / My IP | Kubernetes API Server communication (Day 15+). |\n| **30000 - 32767**| TCP | 0.0.0.0/0 | Kubernetes NodePort Service external access range. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Production Docker Installation (Ubuntu 22.04 / 24.04)\n\nFollow the official Docker repository installation rather than the outdated distro packages (`docker.io`):\n\n```bash\n# Step 1: Update existing system packages\nsudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y\n\n# Step 2: Install required transport, CA, and security utilities\nsudo apt install -y ca-certificates curl gnupg lsb-release\n\n# Step 3: Add Docker's official GPG key securely to keyrings\nsudo install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings\ncurl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg\nsudo chmod a+r /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg\n\n# Step 4: Set up the official Docker APT repository\necho \"deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.gpg] https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu $(lsb_release -cs) stable\" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list > /dev/null\n\n# Step 5: Install Docker Engine, CLI, Containerd, and Compose plugins\nsudo apt update\nsudo apt install -y docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io docker-buildx-plugin docker-compose-plugin\n\n# Step 6: Verify systemd service status\nsudo systemctl status docker --no-pager\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Crucial Post-Installation Steps\n\n### A. Run Docker Without Sudo (Manage Docker as Non-Root)\nBy default, the Docker daemon binds to a Unix socket owned by `root`. Adding your user to the `docker` group eliminates the need to prefix commands with `sudo`:\n\n```bash\n# Add current user to docker group\nsudo usermod -aG docker $USER\n\n# Apply group changes immediately without logging out\nnewgrp docker\n\n# Verify permission\ndocker run hello-world\n```\n\n### B. Configure Docker Daemon for Production (`/etc/docker/daemon.json`)\nConfigure log rotation and the systemd cgroup driver (mandatory for Kubernetes):\n\n```bash\nsudo mkdir -p /etc/docker\nsudo tee /etc/docker/daemon.json <<EOF\n{\n  \"exec-opts\": [\"native.cgroupdriver=systemd\"],\n  \"log-driver\": \"json-file\",\n  \"log-opts\": {\n    \"max-size\": \"100m\",\n    \"max-file\": \"3\"\n  },\n  \"storage-driver\": \"overlay2\"\n}\nEOF\n\nsudo systemctl daemon-reload\nsudo systemctl restart docker\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Verification & Health Check Commands\n\n```bash\n# Check Docker client & server engine versions\ndocker version\n\n# Display comprehensive system-wide Docker engine info\ndocker info\n\n# List active containers\ndocker ps\n\n# Inspect containerd runtime status\nsudo systemctl status containerd\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Interview & Real-World Troubleshooting (Q&A)\n\n### Q1: Why do we use `docker-ce` instead of `apt install docker.io`?\n> **Answer:** `docker.io` is maintained by the Ubuntu/Debian distribution maintainers and is frequently months or years behind the upstream Docker releases. `docker-ce` (Community Edition) comes directly from Docker Inc., containing the latest performance patches, security updates, and full compatibility with Kubernetes CRI tools.\n\n### Q2: Why is `systemd` cgroup driver mandatory when preparing for Kubernetes?\n> **Answer:** On modern Linux systems, systemd is the init system that manages all system resources using cgroups. If Docker uses `cgroupfs` while the OS uses `systemd`, there are two cgroup managers fighting over memory and CPU boundaries. This causes node instability under heavy load. Setting `\"native.cgroupdriver=systemd\"` aligns Docker with the host OS.\n\n### Q3: What happens if Docker disk usage exceeds 90%? How do you fix it?\n> **Answer:** Docker daemon may freeze or fail to create container layers. Clear unused images, stopped containers, and build cache using:\n> `docker system prune -a --volumes`\n",
