    "rawMarkdown": "# Day 04: Docker Basics - Image Commands & Layer Management\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Course & Topic Overview\n- **Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)\n- **Module:** Docker Image Architecture & CLI Commands\n- **Key Focus:** Read-Only Layers, Storage Drivers, Registries, and Image Manipulation\n\n---\n\n## 1. What is a Docker Image?\n\nA **Docker Image** is an immutable (read-only) package containing everything needed to run an application: operating system libraries, dependencies, application binaries, and configuration metadata. Containers are writable runtime instances launched from these static blueprints.\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    subgraph \"Docker Hub / Registry\"\n        RemoteImg[\"nginx:alpine (Remote)\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Docker Host (Local Daemon)\"\n        RemoteImg -->|docker pull| LocalImg[\"Local Image Cache<br/>(Read-Only Layers)\"]\n        LocalImg -->|docker run| C1[\"Container 1 (Writable Layer)\"]\n        LocalImg -->|docker run| C2[\"Container 2 (Writable Layer)\"]\n        LocalImg -->|docker run| C3[\"Container 3 (Writable Layer)\"]\n    end\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Docker Image Full Reference Anatomy\n\nEvery Docker image name follows a standardized hierarchical naming convention:\n\n| Component | Example | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Registry** | `docker.io` (Default) | Domain of the artifact registry (Docker Hub, AWS ECR, Quay.io, GHCR). |\n| **Namespace / User** | `library` (Official) | Account, organization, or team namespace. `library` is reserved for official Docker images. |\n| **Repository** | `nginx` | The specific application or base OS name. |\n| **Tag** | `:1.25-alpine` | Specific version/variant pointer. If omitted, Docker defaults to `:latest`. |\n| **Digest** | `@sha256:abc...` | Cryptographically verified, immutable content hash of the manifest. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Architecture: Layered File System (UnionFS / Overlay2)\n\nDocker images are not monolithic binary blobs. Instead, they are built as a series of stacked, read-only layers. Each layer represents an instruction in a Dockerfile (such as installing a package or copying a file):\n\n```mermaid\ngraph BT\n    L1[\"Layer 1: Base Ubuntu OS (28 MB) - Read-Only\"]\n    L2[\"Layer 2: apt update & python3 (45 MB) - Read-Only\"]\n    L3[\"Layer 3: pip install flask (15 MB) - Read-Only\"]\n    L4[\"Layer 4: COPY app.py (2 KB) - Read-Only\"]\n    C[\"Container Layer: Read/Write (Ephemeral)\"]\n\n    L1 --> L2\n    L2 --> L3\n    L3 --> L4\n    L4 --> C\n```\n\n- **Layer Deduplication:** If 10 images share the same Ubuntu 22.04 base layer, Docker stores it once on disk and shares it across all 10 images.\n- **Content-Addressable Storage:** When pulling or building, Docker checks whether the SHA-256 hash of a layer already exists locally; if yes, it skips downloading.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Comprehensive Docker Image CLI Masterclass\n\n### A. Pulling Images (`docker pull`)\n```bash\n# Pull latest tag by default\ndocker pull ubuntu\n\n# Pull specific version tag (Best Practice)\ndocker pull ubuntu:22.04\n\n# Pull by immutable sha256 digest (Ensures 100% reproducible deployments)\ndocker pull alpine@sha256:c5b1261d6d3e43071626931fc004f70149baeba2c8ec672bd49f277b3b5e1d4b\n```\n\n### B. Listing Images (`docker images` / `docker image ls`)\n```bash\n# Standard listing\ndocker images\n\n# List all images including intermediate layers\ndocker images -a\n\n# Custom formatted output (Useful for automation scripts)\ndocker images --format \"table {{.Repository}}\\t{{.Tag}}\\t{{.ID}}\\t{{.Size}}\"\n\n# Filter dangling images (<none>:<none>)\ndocker images -f \"dangling=true\"\n```\n\n### C. Inspecting Image Metadata (`docker image inspect`)\nReturns the complete low-level JSON configuration including OS architecture, environment variables, default exposed ports, and volume mount points:\n```bash\n# Deep JSON dump of image config\ndocker image inspect nginx:alpine\n\n# Extract specific field using Go templates\ndocker image inspect --format='{{.Config.ExposedPorts}}' nginx:alpine\ndocker image inspect --format='{{.Architecture}}' ubuntu:22.04\n```\n\n### D. Viewing Image Layer History (`docker image history`)\n```bash\n# View build steps, layer sizes, and created timestamps\ndocker image history nginx:alpine --no-trunc\n```\n\n### E. Tagging Images (`docker tag`)\nCreates an alias pointing to the same underlying image ID without duplicating storage:\n```bash\n# Syntax: docker tag <SOURCE_IMAGE>[:TAG] <TARGET_IMAGE>[:TAG]\ndocker tag ubuntu:22.04 mycompany-registry.com/team-dev/ubuntu:v22.04\n\n# Notice that both have identical IMAGE IDs!\ndocker images\n```\n\n### F. Deleting Images (`docker rmi` / `docker image rm`)\n```bash\n# Remove image by name:tag (Untags if other tags point to the same ID)\ndocker rmi mycompany-registry.com/team-dev/ubuntu:v22.04\n\n# Remove image by short Image ID\ndocker rmi 35a88802559d\n\n# Force removal (Caution: only if safe)\ndocker rmi -f <IMAGE_ID>\n```\n\n### G. Disk Cleanup (`docker image prune`)\n```bash\n# Remove only dangling images (<none>:<none>)\ndocker image prune\n\n# Remove ALL unused images (not referenced by any running or stopped container)\ndocker image prune -a --force\n```\n\n### H. Offline Image Transfer (`docker save` & `docker load`)\nCrucial for air-gapped production servers with zero internet connectivity:\n```bash\n# On internet-connected machine: Export image to compressed tar\ndocker save nginx:alpine | gzip > nginx-alpine.tar.gz\n\n# Transfer via scp to air-gapped host\nscp nginx-alpine.tar.gz user@airgapped-server:/tmp/\n\n# On air-gapped host: Load image back into Docker daemon\ndocker load -i /tmp/nginx-alpine.tar.gz\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Dangling Images vs Unused Images\n\n| Concept | What It Means | How It Is Created | Cleanup Command |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Dangling Image** | An image with no repository or tag (`<none>:<none>`). | Rebuilding an image with the same tag as an existing one. | `docker image prune` |\n| **Unused Image** | An image with a valid tag, but no container is currently using it. | Pulling an image and never running a container from it. | `docker image prune -a` |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Interview & Real-World Troubleshooting (Q&A)\n\n### Q1: What is the difference between \"Untagged\" and \"Deleted\" during `docker rmi`?\n> **Answer:** Untagging removes the tag reference pointer. Layers are only deleted from physical storage when the LAST tag pointing to the image is removed and no containers (running or stopped) depend on those layers.\n\n### Q2: What is the difference between an Image ID and an Image Digest?\n> **Answer:** An Image ID is a local sha256 hash calculated from the image's config JSON on the Docker host. An Image Digest is an immutable cryptographic hash of the image manifest computed by the container registry (e.g. Docker Hub), guaranteeing identical contents across different machines.\n\n### Q3: If 5 different images are built `FROM ubuntu:22.04`, does Docker duplicate the Ubuntu layers?\n> **Answer:** No. Docker uses Content-Addressable Storage (CAS). The Ubuntu layers are stored once in `/var/lib/docker/overlay2` and referenced by all 5 images, consuming zero additional disk space.\n\n### Q4: How do you deploy a Docker image to an air-gapped server with no internet access?\n> **Answer:** Export the image to a tarball using `docker save -o image.tar <image_name>`, copy it over via USB or `scp`, and import it on the target host using `docker load -i image.tar`.\n",
