    "rawMarkdown": "# Day 05: Docker Basics - Container Lifecycle & Essential Commands\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Course & Topic Overview\n- **Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)\n- **Module:** Container Lifecycle, Process Isolation & Runtime Commands\n- **Key Concepts:** Service vs Raw Containers, PID 1, `docker attach` vs `docker exec`, Environment Variables (`-e`), Host Process Visibility\n\n---\n\n## 1. Container Classification: Service vs Raw Containers\n\nA very common beginner misconception: running `docker run -d ubuntu` results in a container that immediately exits with code 0. Why?\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    subgraph \"Service Containers (e.g., Nginx, MySQL, Apache)\"\n        S1[\"docker run -d --name=web nginx\"] --> S2[\"PID 1 is a long-running Daemon (nginx: master)\"]\n        S2 --> S3[\"Container remains in RUNNING state continuously\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Raw OS Containers (e.g., Ubuntu, Debian, Alpine)\"\n        R1[\"docker run -d ubuntu\"] --> R2[\"PID 1 is /bin/bash (Requires STDIN)\"]\n        R2 --> R3[\"No terminal attached -> bash exits immediately (Code 0)\"]\n        R4[\"docker run -itd --name=con1 ubuntu\"] --> R5[\"Allocates Pseudo-TTY (-t) & keeps STDIN open (-i)\"]\n        R5 --> R6[\"Container remains RUNNING in background!\"]\n    end\n```\n\n| Type | Examples | Default PID 1 Command | Correct Launch Syntax | Behavior |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Service Container** | `nginx`, `httpd`, `mysql`, `redis` | `nginx -g 'daemon off;'` | `docker run -d --name=web nginx` | Runs as background daemon. |\n| **Raw Container** | `ubuntu`, `debian`, `alpine`, `centos` | `/bin/bash` or `/bin/sh` | `docker run -itd --name=con1 ubuntu` | Requires TTY + STDIN to stay alive. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Anatomy of a Running Container\n\nWhen a container starts, Docker leverages Linux kernel features to instantiate 4 foundational isolation boundaries:\n1. **PID (Process ID Namespace):** Private process tree starting at PID 1 inside the container (isolated from host PID space).\n2. **VNIC (Virtual Ethernet):** Virtual network interface pair connecting the container to the `docker0` Linux bridge.\n3. **IP Address:** Dedicated private IP address allocated dynamically by Docker IPAM (e.g., `172.17.0.2`).\n4. **FileSystem:** Stacked read-only image layers plus a thin read-write container layer (Overlay2).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Deep Dive: `docker attach` vs `docker exec`\n\n| Criteria | `docker attach` | `docker exec -it` |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Target Process** | Attaches to the existing primary process (PID 1). | Spawns a brand NEW sub-process in the container namespaces. |\n| **Typing `exit`** | Kills PID 1 -> **CONTAINER STOPS IMMEDIATELY!** | Terminates only the sub-shell; container keeps running. |\n| **Safe Detach Key**| `Ctrl + P` then `Ctrl + Q` (Escape sequence). | Just type `exit` or `Ctrl + D`. |\n| **Applicability** | Only works when PID 1 is an interactive shell (Raw). | Works on ALL containers (Raw and Service containers). |\n| **Production Use**| Discouraged (High risk of stopping production containers).| Industry Standard for debugging and shell access. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Container CLI Masterclass\n\n### A. Launching Containers (`docker run`)\n```bash\n# Launch service container in background (detached)\ndocker run -d --name=web nginx:latest\n\n# Launch raw OS container in background with interactive pseudo-TTY\ndocker run -itd --name=con1 ubuntu\n\n# Launch with environment variables\ndocker run -d --name=database -e MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=redhat mysql:5.6\n\n# Launch and automatically remove container on exit (--rm)\ndocker run --rm -it alpine sh\n```\n\n### B. Executing Commands Inside Containers (`docker exec`)\n```bash\n# Open an interactive bash shell\ndocker exec -it con1 bash\n\n# Run a single command without entering shell\ndocker exec web nginx -v\ndocker exec database env\n```\n\n### C. Monitoring Processes & Host Inspection (`docker top` & `ps -ef`)\nContainers share the host kernel. You can see container processes on the host:\n```bash\n# View container processes and their corresponding Host PIDs\ndocker top web\n\n# On the host: Find the exact host PID of the container process\nps -ef | grep nginx\n\n# Demonstrating kernel sharing: Killing host PID stops the container\nkill -9 <HOST_PID>\ndocker ps   # 'web' container will now be stopped!\n```\n\n### D. Container Inspection & Host Storage (`docker inspect` & `/var/lib/docker`)\n```bash\n# Detailed JSON inspection of container state, IP, and mount points\ndocker inspect con1\n\n# Extract specific JSON values\ndocker inspect --format='{{.NetworkSettings.IPAddress}}' con1\ndocker inspect --format='{{.State.Pid}}' con1\n\n# View physical host directory where container configs & logs live\nls /var/lib/docker/containers/<CONTAINER_ID>/\n```\n\n### E. Lifecycle Control & Batch Cleanup\n```bash\n# Stop, start, and restart\ndocker stop con1\ndocker start con1\ndocker restart con1\n\n# Remove stopped container\ndocker rm con1\n\n# Force remove running container\ndocker rm -f con1\n\n# Power Command: Stop ALL containers at once\ndocker stop $(docker ps -q -a)\n\n# Power Command: Delete ALL containers at once\ndocker rm $(docker ps -q -a)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Environment Variables & Troubleshooting\n\nMany service images require mandatory environment variables to initialize properly (e.g. databases):\n```bash\n# What happens if you forget required env vars?\ndocker run -d --name=broken-db mysql:5.6\n\n# Container immediately exits! Check the logs:\ndocker logs broken-db\n# Output: [ERROR] Database is uninitialized and password option is not specified.\n#         You need to specify MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD!\n\n# Fix: Provide the required variable via -e\ndocker run -d --name=fixed-db -e MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=secret mysql:5.6\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Interview & Real-World Troubleshooting (Q&A)\n\n### Q1: Why does `docker run -d ubuntu` stop immediately after starting?\n> **Answer:** Because Ubuntu's default command is `/bin/bash`, which requires an active interactive input stream (`STDIN`). Without `-it`, bash exits with code 0 because it reaches EOF immediately. To keep it alive in the background, use `docker run -itd ubuntu`.\n\n### Q2: What is the critical danger of using `docker attach` instead of `docker exec`?\n> **Answer:** `docker attach` binds directly to PID 1. If you type `exit`, PID 1 terminates, which immediately kills the entire container. `docker exec` spawns a separate child process, so typing `exit` only closes that session while keeping the container and its service running.\n\n### Q3: How do you detach from a container connected via `docker attach` without stopping it?\n> **Answer:** Press the escape sequence: `Ctrl + P` followed by `Ctrl + Q`.\n\n### Q4: Is a container process visible in the host OS process table?\n> **Answer:** Yes! Containers are not virtual machines with a separate guest kernel; they are isolated processes running directly on the host Linux kernel. Running `ps -ef` on the host will show the container's process, and sending `kill -9` from the host will terminate it.\n",
    "mermaid": "graph TD\n    subgraph \"Service Containers (e.g., Nginx, MySQL, Apache)\"\n        S1[\"docker run -d --name=web nginx\"] --> S2[\"PID 1 is a long-running Daemon (nginx: master)\"]\n        S2 --> S3[\"Container remains in RUNNING state continuously\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Raw OS Containers (e.g., Ubuntu, Debian, Alpine)\"\n        R1[\"docker run -d ubuntu\"] --> R2[\"PID 1 is /bin/bash (Requires STDIN)\"]\n        R2 --> R3[\"No terminal attached -> bash exits immediately (Code 0)\"]\n        R4[\"docker run -itd --name=con1 ubuntu\"] --> R5[\"Allocates Pseudo-TTY (-t) & keeps STDIN open (-i)\"]\n        R5 --> R6[\"Container remains RUNNING in background!\"]\n    end",
