    "rawMarkdown": "# Day 07: Dockerfile & Image Build - Declarative Images, Best Practices & Quiz Recap\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Course & Topic Overview\n- **Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)\n- **Module:** Image Construction, Dockerfile Primitives, Caching & Quiz Recap\n- **Key Concepts:** `docker commit` vs `docker build`, Dockerfile Instructions, `CMD` vs `ENTRYPOINT`, Exec vs Shell Form, `.dockerignore`\n\n---\n\n## 1. Two Approaches to Creating Docker Images\n\n| Criteria | `docker commit` (Imperative) | `Dockerfile` & `docker build` (Declarative) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Workflow** | Start container -> manually configure -> commit to new image. | Write declarative instructions in a Dockerfile -> run build. |\n| **Reproducibility** | Poor (Black box; changes are undocumented). | 100% Reproducible & version-controlled in Git. |\n| **Layer Efficiency**| Bloated (Captures temporary files, cache, bash history). | Optimized (Multi-stage builds, cache layers, minimal footprint). |\n| **Production Status**| Strictly an **ANTI-PATTERN**. | **Industry Standard** for CI/CD & DevOps. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Core Dockerfile Instructions Reference\n\n| Instruction | Purpose | Creates New Layer? | Best Practice Example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`FROM`** | Defines the base operating system or runtime image. | Yes | `FROM ubuntu:22.04` |\n| **`RUN`** | Executes commands during build time to install software. | Yes | `RUN apt update && apt install -y curl && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*` |\n| **`COPY`** | Copies files/directories from host build context into image. | Yes | `COPY ./src /app` |\n| **`ADD`** | Like COPY, but auto-extracts tar files & supports remote URLs. | Yes | `ADD archive.tar.gz /opt/` (Prefer `COPY` for simple files). |\n| **`WORKDIR`**| Sets active working directory for RUN, CMD, and ENTRYPOINT. | No (Metadata) | `WORKDIR /app` |\n| **`ENV`** | Sets environment variables persistent in build and runtime. | No (Metadata) | `ENV PORT=8080 APP_ENV=production` |\n| **`EXPOSE`** | Documents which network ports the container listens on. | No (Metadata) | `EXPOSE 80 443` (Documentation only; does not publish). |\n| **`ENTRYPOINT`** | Sets the primary executable container runs on startup. | No (Metadata) | `ENTRYPOINT [\"nginx\"]` |\n| **`CMD`** | Provides default parameters or fallback command for container. | No (Metadata) | `CMD [\"-g\", \"daemon off;\"]` |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Deep Dive: `CMD` vs `ENTRYPOINT` (Shell vs Exec Form)\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    subgraph \"Exec Form (MANDATORY for Production)\"\n        E1[\"CMD [\\\"nginx\\\", \\\"-g\\\", \\\"daemon off;\\\"]\"] --> E2[\"Runs directly as PID 1\"]\n        E2 --> E3[\"Receives SIGTERM/SIGINT signals properly for graceful shutdown\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Shell Form (Avoid in Production)\"\n        S1[\"CMD nginx -g 'daemon off;'\"] --> S2[\"Wrapped in /bin/sh -c (sh becomes PID 1)\"]\n        S2 --> S3[\"Child processes do NOT receive shutdown signals -> abrupt termination!\"]\n    end\n```\n\n- **The Synergy Pattern:** `ENTRYPOINT` defines the immutable executable. `CMD` provides default arguments. When both are present, `CMD` is appended to `ENTRYPOINT`: `ENTRYPOINT + CMD`.\n- **Runtime Flexibility:** If a user runs `docker run myapp --verbose`, the `--verbose` flag overrides `CMD` but leaves `ENTRYPOINT` untouched.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Layer Caching & Build Context Optimization\n\nDocker caches intermediate layers by hashing the instruction and file contents. To maximize build speed:\n1. **Order Matters:** Place rarely-changing instructions (like OS updates, package installs) at the TOP of the Dockerfile.\n2. **Put Code at the End:** Place frequently-changing application source code at the BOTTOM so modifications don't invalidate upper cached layers.\n3. **Use `.dockerignore`:** Always add a `.dockerignore` file (excluding `.git`, `node_modules`, `*.log`) to prevent gigabytes of useless files from being sent to the Docker daemon build context.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Step-by-Step Hands-On Build Lab\n\n```bash\n# Step 1: Create a clean workspace\nmkdir -p /tmp/webapp && cd /tmp/webapp\n\n# Step 2: Create a sample web page\necho \"<h1>Welcome to KR Network Cloud Docker Lab!</h1>\" > index.html\n\n# Step 3: Create .dockerignore\ncat <<EOF > .dockerignore\n.git\n*.log\ntmp/\nEOF\n\n# Step 4: Write production-grade Dockerfile\ncat <<EOF > Dockerfile\nFROM ubuntu:22.04\nENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive\nRUN apt update && \\\n    apt install -y nginx && \\\n    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\nCOPY index.html /var/www/html/index.html\nEXPOSE 80\nENTRYPOINT [\"nginx\"]\nCMD [\"-g\", \"daemon off;\"]\nEOF\n\n# Step 5: Build the image with a tag\ndocker build -t kr-webapp:v1.0 .\n\n# Step 6: Run and test the container\ndocker run -d -p 8080:80 --name=my-app kr-webapp:v1.0\ncurl http://localhost:8080\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Course Quiz & Comprehensive Review Questions\n\n### Q1: Why is `docker build` preferred over `docker commit` in enterprise environments?\n> **Answer:** Declarative configuration (`Dockerfile`) ensures complete reproducibility, auditability in Git, and automated integration into CI/CD pipelines, whereas `docker commit` produces opaque black-box images with bloated layers and untracked changes.\n\n### Q2: What is the difference between `COPY` and `ADD` instructions?\n> **Answer:** `COPY` only supports copying local files from the build context. `ADD` additionally supports extracting local tar archives automatically and downloading files from remote URLs (though downloading via `RUN curl` or `wget` is best practice to avoid bloat).\n\n### Q3: Why is the Exec Form (`[\"binary\", \"arg\"]`) preferred over Shell Form for `CMD` and `ENTRYPOINT`?\n> **Answer:** Exec form runs the binary directly as PID 1, allowing it to receive Linux shutdown signals (`SIGTERM`) for graceful termination. Shell form spawns a shell wrapper (`/bin/sh -c`), preventing signals from reaching the application process.\n\n### Q4: Does specifying `EXPOSE 80` in a Dockerfile automatically publish the port to the host?\n> **Answer:** No. `EXPOSE` is purely documentation indicating which port the application listens on. To publish the port to the host network, you must explicitly use the `-p <host_port>:<container_port>` or `-P` flag at runtime.\n",
    "mermaid": "graph TD\n    subgraph \"Exec Form (MANDATORY for Production)\"\n        E1[\"CMD [\\\"nginx\\\", \\\"-g\\\", \\\"daemon off;\\\"]\"] --> E2[\"Runs directly as PID 1\"]\n        E2 --> E3[\"Receives SIGTERM/SIGINT signals properly for graceful shutdown\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Shell Form (Avoid in Production)\"\n        S1[\"CMD nginx -g 'daemon off;'\"] --> S2[\"Wrapped in /bin/sh -c (sh becomes PID 1)\"]\n        S2 --> S3[\"Child processes do NOT receive shutdown signals -> abrupt termination!\"]\n    end",
    "commands": "# BASH\n# Step 1: Create a clean workspace\nmkdir -p /tmp/webapp && cd /tmp/webapp\n\n# Step 2: Create a sample web page\necho \"<h1>Welcome to KR Network Cloud Docker Lab!</h1>\" > index.html\n\n# Step 3: Create .dockerignore\ncat <<EOF > .dockerignore\n.git\n*.log\ntmp/\nEOF\n\n# Step 4: Write production-grade Dockerfile\ncat <<EOF > Dockerfile\nFROM ubuntu:22.04\nENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive\nRUN apt update && \\\n    apt install -y nginx && \\\n    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\nCOPY index.html /var/www/html/index.html\nEXPOSE 80\nENTRYPOINT [\"nginx\"]\nCMD [\"-g\", \"daemon off;\"]\nEOF\n\n# Step 5: Build the image with a tag\ndocker build -t kr-webapp:v1.0 .\n\n# Step 6: Run and test the container\ndocker run -d -p 8080:80 --name=my-app kr-webapp:v1.0\ncurl http://localhost:8080",
