    "title": "Day 08: Day 08: Docker Persistent Storage - Volumes, Bind Mounts & Cloud Disk Lab",
    "cleanTitle": "Day 08: Docker Persistent Storage - Volumes, Bind Mounts & Cloud Disk Lab",
    "duration": "2h 15m",
    "moduleId": 3,
    "moduleName": "Module 3: Storage Drivers & Declarative Image Builds",
    "moduleBadge": "Storage & Build",
    "color": "#F59E0B",
    "icon": "hard-drive",
    "rawMarkdown": "# Day 08: Docker Persistent Storage - Volumes, Bind Mounts & Cloud Disk Lab\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Course & Topic Overview\n- **Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)\n- **Module:** Persistent Storage, Mount Types, Cloud Block Storage & Backup Strategies\n- **Key Concepts:** Ephemeral Layers vs Volumes, Bind Mounts (`-v` vs `--mount`), Read-Only protection (`:ro`), AWS EBS Attachment, Volume Backup/Restore\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Ephemeral Container Problem\n\nBy default, all files created inside a container are stored on its writable container layer (Overlay2). When the container is deleted (`docker rm`), this layer is destroyed. For stateful applications (MySQL, Postgres, Kafka), data MUST survive container termination:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    subgraph \"Host Machine (EC2 / Linux)\"\n        HostDir[\"Host Directory: /data/app<br/>(Bind Mount)\"]\n        DockerVol[\"Docker Managed Storage:<br/>/var/lib/docker/volumes/my-vol/_data<br/>(Named Volume)\"]\n        HostRAM[\"Host Memory (RAM)<br/>(tmpfs mount)\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Docker Container\"\n        C1[\"Container Layer (Overlay2)<br/>EPHEMERAL (Deleted on docker rm)\"]\n        C2[\"/var/lib/mysql<br/>(Mount Target)\"]\n    end\n    DockerVol -->|High Performance / Production| C2\n    HostDir -->|Source Code / Configs| C2\n    HostRAM -->|Sensitive Cache / Non-Persistent| C2\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Storage Mount Types Comparison\n\n| Feature | Docker Volumes (Named) | Bind Mounts | tmpfs Mounts |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Host Location** | Managed by Docker (`/var/lib/docker/volumes/`). | Any user-specified host path. | Host system memory (RAM). |\n| **Management** | Docker CLI (`docker volume ...`). | Host file system utilities. | Linux kernel memory manager. |\n| **Portability** | High (Can use cloud drivers like AWS EFS/EBS). | Low (Depends on host directory structure). | N/A (In-memory only). |\n| **Performance** | Native disk speed (bypasses Overlay2 CoW). | Native disk speed (bypasses Overlay2 CoW). | Extremely fast (RAM speed). |\n| **Primary Use Case** | Databases, production persistent data. | Source code live reload in development. | Sensitive credentials, caching. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Docker Volumes CLI Masterclass\n\n```bash\n# Create a named volume\ndocker volume create pg-data\n\n# List all local volumes\ndocker volume ls\n\n# Inspect volume metadata and physical host path\ndocker volume inspect pg-data\n\n# Mount volume to container using -v flag (Syntax: -v <vol_name>:<container_path>)\ndocker run -d --name=my-db -v pg-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data postgres:15\n\n# Mount volume using modern --mount syntax (Preferred for clarity)\ndocker run -d --name=my-db2 --mount source=pg-data,target=/var/lib/postgresql/data postgres:15\n\n# Clean up unused volumes (Dangerous: deletes all unattached volumes!)\ndocker volume prune --force\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Bind Mounts: Development & Read-Only Protection\n\n```bash\n# Mount host directory for real-time development live reload\ndocker run -d --name=dev-web -p 80:80 -v $(pwd)/html:/usr/share/nginx/html nginx\n\n# Mount configuration file as READ-ONLY (:ro) to prevent container tampering\ndocker run -d --name=secure-web -v /etc/nginx/nginx.conf:/etc/nginx/nginx.conf:ro nginx\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Cloud Lab: Attaching External AWS EBS Disk to Docker\n\nIn cloud environments, high-availability databases require dedicated block storage volumes (AWS EBS) formatted and mounted to containers:\n\n```bash\n# Step 1: Check attached block devices on EC2\nlsblk\n\n# Step 2: Format the new raw EBS volume with ext4\nsudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/xvdf\n\n# Step 3: Create mount point and mount disk\nsudo mkdir -p /mnt/ebs-cloud-storage\nsudo mount /dev/xvdf /mnt/ebs-cloud-storage\n\n# Step 4: Verify filesystem disk space\ndf -h /mnt/ebs-cloud-storage\n\n# Step 5: Launch production database using cloud storage\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name=enterprise-db \\\n  -v /mnt/ebs-cloud-storage:/var/lib/mysql \\\n  -e MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=EnterprisePassword123 \\\n  mysql:5.6\n\n# Step 6: Test persistence: Stop & delete container\ndocker rm -f enterprise-db\n\n# Step 7: Launch a NEW container with the same volume mount\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name=enterprise-db-recovered \\\n  -v /mnt/ebs-cloud-storage:/var/lib/mysql \\\n  mysql:5.6\n\n# All databases and tables remain 100% intact!\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Automated Volume Backup & Restore Technique\n\nHow to safely back up a Docker named volume without stopping dependent containers:\n\n```bash\n# Backup: Launch a temporary alpine container to tar the volume contents\ndocker run --rm \\\n  --volumes-from enterprise-db-recovered \\\n  -v $(pwd):/backup \\\n  alpine tar cvf /backup/db_backup_$(date +%Y%m%d).tar /var/lib/mysql\n\n# Restore: Extract backup archive into a fresh volume\ndocker run --rm \\\n  -v fresh-db-data:/var/lib/mysql \\\n  -v $(pwd):/backup \\\n  alpine tar xvf /backup/db_backup_20260920.tar -C /\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Interview & Real-World Troubleshooting (Q&A)\n\n### Q1: What is the key difference between a Docker Volume and a Bind Mount?\n> **Answer:** Docker Volumes are managed exclusively by Docker within `/var/lib/docker/volumes` and are isolated from the host OS directory structure. Bind Mounts map arbitrary host directories directly into the container and rely on host paths and permissions.\n\n### Q2: How do you mount a host configuration file so the container cannot modify it?\n> **Answer:** Append `:ro` to the volume flag, e.g., `-v /etc/nginx/nginx.conf:/etc/nginx/nginx.conf:ro` or `--mount type=bind,source=/etc/nginx/nginx.conf,target=/etc/nginx/nginx.conf,readonly`.\n\n### Q3: If you bind mount an empty host folder to an existing non-empty container folder, what happens?\n> **Answer:** The host directory overrides the container directory. Any pre-existing files inside the container directory become hidden/shadowed for as long as the mount is active.\n\n### Q4: How do you back up a production Docker volume?\n> **Answer:** Launch a temporary disposable container using `--volumes-from <container>` or `-v <volume_name>:/data`, and run `tar cvf /backup/backup.tar /data` to write the compressed archive to a mounted host directory.\n",
    "mermaid": "graph TD\n    subgraph \"Host Machine (EC2 / Linux)\"\n        HostDir[\"Host Directory: /data/app<br/>(Bind Mount)\"]\n        DockerVol[\"Docker Managed Storage:<br/>/var/lib/docker/volumes/my-vol/_data<br/>(Named Volume)\"]\n        HostRAM[\"Host Memory (RAM)<br/>(tmpfs mount)\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Docker Container\"\n        C1[\"Container Layer (Overlay2)<br/>EPHEMERAL (Deleted on docker rm)\"]\n        C2[\"/var/lib/mysql<br/>(Mount Target)\"]\n    end\n    DockerVol -->|High Performance / Production| C2\n    HostDir -->|Source Code / Configs| C2\n    HostRAM -->|Sensitive Cache / Non-Persistent| C2",
