    "rawMarkdown": "# Day 11: Advanced Docker Networking - Host, None, Macvlan & IPvlan Drivers\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Course & Topic Overview\n- **Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)\n- **Module:** Advanced Network Drivers & Layer 2/3 Virtualization\n- **Key Concepts:** Host Network (`--network host`), None Network (`--network none`), 2-Tier Stack Deployment, Macvlan vs IPvlan Architecture\n\n---\n\n## 1. Docker Network Drivers Comprehensive Matrix\n\n| Driver | Network Namespace Isolation | Has Own IP? | Port Forwarding Needed? | Typical Production Use Case |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **`bridge`** | Isolated (`veth` pair) | Yes (Private Subnet) | Yes (`-p`) | Standard Microservices & multi-container stacks. |\n| **`host`** | Shared with Host | No (Uses Host IP) | No (Binds direct to host) | High-performance network services (VoIP, streaming). |\n| **`none`** | Completely Isolated | No (Only `127.0.0.1`) | No (No external access) | Air-gapped batch processing, security encryption. |\n| **`macvlan`** | Isolated (Direct to physical L2) | Yes (LAN IP + Unique MAC) | No | Legacy apps requiring direct physical switch integration. |\n| **`ipvlan`** | Isolated (Direct to physical L2) | Yes (LAN IP + Shared MAC) | No | High container density avoiding switch MAC table limits. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Deep Dive: Host & None Networks\n\n### A. Host Network (`--network host`)\nBypasses Docker's network virtualization entirely. The container binds directly to host ports, delivering zero-overhead native network throughput:\n\n```bash\n# Launch Nginx on host network\ndocker run -d --name=host-web --network host nginx\n\n# Verify on host: Nginx is listening directly on host port 80!\nsudo netstat -tunlp | grep 80\n\n# Port Conflict Demonstration:\n# Trying to run a second container on port 80 fails:\ndocker run -d --name=host-web2 --network host nginx\ndocker logs host-web2\n# Output: [emerg] bind() to 0.0.0.0:80 failed (98: Address already in use)\n```\n\n### B. None Network (`--network none`)\n```bash\n# Launch isolated container with zero network interfaces\ndocker run -it --rm --network none alpine ip addr\n# Output: 1: lo: <LOOPBACK,UP,LOWER_UP> mtu 65536 ... inet 127.0.0.1/8\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Production Lab: 2-Tier Application (WordPress + MySQL)\n\nDeploying a secure two-tier application where the database is isolated and only the web frontend is exposed to external traffic:\n\n```bash\n# Step 1: Create dedicated application network\ndocker network create --driver bridge app-tier-net\n\n# Step 2: Deploy MySQL database container (No ports exposed externally!)\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name=dbserver \\\n  --network app-tier-net \\\n  -e MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=AdminPassword123 \\\n  -e MYSQL_USER=wpuser \\\n  -e MYSQL_PASSWORD=wppassword \\\n  -e MYSQL_DATABASE=wordpress_db \\\n  mysql:5.6\n\n# Step 3: Deploy WordPress frontend connecting via embedded DNS\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name=wordpress-app \\\n  --network app-tier-net \\\n  -p 8080:80 \\\n  -e WORDPRESS_DB_HOST=dbserver \\\n  -e WORDPRESS_DB_USER=wpuser \\\n  -e WORDPRESS_DB_PASSWORD=wppassword \\\n  -e WORDPRESS_DB_NAME=wordpress_db \\\n  wordpress:latest\n\n# Step 4: Access in browser via http://<HOST_IP>:8080\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Under the Hood: Macvlan vs IPvlan\n\nWhen containers need to appear as legitimate physical endpoints on the company's local area network (LAN):\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    subgraph \"Physical Network / Corporate Router\"\n        Router[\"Physical Switch / Router (192.168.1.1)\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Macvlan Driver (Unique MAC per Container)\"\n        Router --- M1[\"Container 1: IP: 192.168.1.10 | MAC: 02:42:c0:a8:01:0a\"]\n        Router --- M2[\"Container 2: IP: 192.168.1.11 | MAC: 02:42:c0:a8:01:0b\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"IPvlan Driver (Shared Host MAC)\"\n        Router --- I1[\"Container 3: IP: 192.168.1.20 | Host MAC: 00:0c:29:ab:cd:ef\"]\n        Router --- I2[\"Container 4: IP: 192.168.1.21 | Host MAC: 00:0c:29:ab:cd:ef\"]\n    end\n```\n\n```bash\n# Macvlan Network Creation\ndocker network create -d macvlan \\\n  --subnet=192.168.1.0/24 \\\n  --gateway=192.168.1.1 \\\n  -o parent=eth0 \\\n  corp-macvlan\n\n# Assign static IP to container\ndocker run -itd --name=mac-app --network corp-macvlan --ip 192.168.1.15 alpine\n\n# IPvlan Network Creation (Shares parent interface MAC)\ndocker network create -d ipvlan \\\n  --subnet=192.168.1.0/24 \\\n  --gateway=192.168.1.1 \\\n  -o parent=eth0 \\\n  corp-ipvlan\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Interview & Real-World Troubleshooting (Q&A)\n\n### Q1: What is the architectural difference between `--network host` and `--network bridge`?\n> **Answer:** In `--network host`, the container shares the host network namespace directly with zero network isolation or NAT overhead. In `--network bridge`, the container gets its own isolated network namespace and communicates through the `docker0` bridge via NAT.\n\n### Q2: Why would an enterprise prefer IPvlan over Macvlan?\n> **Answer:** Macvlan assigns a unique MAC address to every container, which can exhaust the MAC learning table of physical network switches and is blocked by hypervisors/clouds (like AWS EC2). IPvlan assigns distinct IPs while sharing the single physical MAC of the host NIC, eliminating MAC exhaustion.\n\n### Q3: Can the Docker host communicate directly with a Macvlan container running on the same host?\n> **Answer:** By default, no. The Linux kernel forbids communication between the Macvlan parent interface and child interfaces on the same physical link for security isolation. A dedicated macvlan shim bridge on the host is required for host-to-container communication.\n\n### Q4: Which network driver should you use for executing a high-security cryptographic key generator?\n> **Answer:** Use `--network none`. It restricts the container exclusively to the loopback interface with no external connectivity, ensuring air-gapped security.\n",
    "mermaid": "graph TD\n    subgraph \"Physical Network / Corporate Router\"\n        Router[\"Physical Switch / Router (192.168.1.1)\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Macvlan Driver (Unique MAC per Container)\"\n        Router --- M1[\"Container 1: IP: 192.168.1.10 | MAC: 02:42:c0:a8:01:0a\"]\n        Router --- M2[\"Container 2: IP: 192.168.1.11 | MAC: 02:42:c0:a8:01:0b\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"IPvlan Driver (Shared Host MAC)\"\n        Router --- I1[\"Container 3: IP: 192.168.1.20 | Host MAC: 00:0c:29:ab:cd:ef\"]\n        Router --- I2[\"Container 4: IP: 192.168.1.21 | Host MAC: 00:0c:29:ab:cd:ef\"]\n    end",
