    "rawMarkdown": "# Day 12: Dockerfile Deep Dive - Multi-Stage Builds, ARG vs ENV & Hardening\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Course & Topic Overview\n- **Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)\n- **Module:** Advanced Image Construction, Multi-Stage Builds & Container Security\n- **Key Concepts:** Multi-Stage Compilation, `ARG` vs `ENV`, Non-Root Execution (`USER`), Layer Minimization, BuildKit Cache Management\n\n---\n\n## 1. Multi-Stage Builds: The 1GB to 15MB Revolution\n\nTraditional Docker builds packaged compilers, SDKs, and build tools into the production image, creating bloated images vulnerable to security exploits. Multi-Stage builds solve this:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    subgraph \"Stage 1: Build Environment (Heavy: 850 MB)\"\n        B1[\"Base: golang:alpine\"] --> B2[\"Source Code + Compiler\"]\n        B2 --> B3[\"Compile Binary: /app/myapp\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Stage 2: Production Runtime (Ultra-Light: 12 MB)\"\n        R1[\"Base: alpine:latest\"] --> R2[\"COPY --from=builder /app/myapp\"]\n        R2 --> R3[\"Minimal Attack Surface!\"]\n    end\n    B3 -.->|Only Binary Transferred| R2\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Deep Comparison: ARG vs ENV\n\n| Feature | ARG (Build-Time Variable) | ENV (Environment Variable) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Scope** | Available ONLY during `docker build`. | Available during build AND inside running containers. |\n| **Persistence in Container** | \u274c **NO** (Discarded after build finishes) | \u2705 **YES** (Accessible via `env` or application code) |\n| **CLI Override** | `--build-arg KEY=VALUE` | `docker run -e KEY=VALUE` |\n| **Typical Use Case** | Selecting package versions or git commit SHAs. | Database URLs, runtime configuration, ports. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Production Dockerfile Hardening Best Practices\n\n1. **Non-Root Execution:** Never run as root in production! Create a dedicated non-root user and switch using `USER appuser`.\n2. **Layer Minimization:** Combine multiple `RUN` instructions into a single command using `&&` and clean up package caches to reduce intermediate layers.\n3. **Prefer COPY over ADD:** Avoid `ADD` for remote files; use `RUN curl ... && rm ...` so temporary archives do not persist in layer history.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Complete Production Multi-Stage Dockerfile\n\n```docker\n# --- STAGE 1: Builder ---\nFROM golang:1.20-alpine AS builder\n\n# Install build dependencies\nRUN apk update && apk add --no-cache git\n\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY go.mod go.sum* ./\nRUN go mod download\n\nCOPY . .\n# Statically compile binary with no external C dependencies\nRUN CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux go build -ldflags=\"-w -s\" -o /bin/api-server .\n\n# --- STAGE 2: Hardened Minimal Runtime ---\nFROM alpine:3.18\n\n# Create unprivileged security user\nRUN addgroup -S appgroup && adduser -S appuser -G appgroup\n\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=builder /bin/api-server /app/api-server\n\n# Switch to non-root user\nUSER appuser\n\nEXPOSE 8080\nENTRYPOINT [\"/app/api-server\"]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. BuildKit Cache Maintenance\n\n```bash\n# Clean up dangling and unused BuildKit build cache\ndocker builder prune\n\n# Force purge ALL build cache (reclaims dozens of gigabytes)\ndocker builder prune -a --force\n\n# Remove all unused images and builder cache together\ndocker system prune -a --volumes\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Interview & Real-World Troubleshooting (Q&A)\n\n### Q1: What is the primary architectural benefit of Multi-Stage Builds?\n> **Answer:** Multi-stage builds separate the build environment from the final runtime image, copying only the compiled artifacts. This reduces image size from over 1GB to a few megabytes and drastically cuts down security vulnerabilities.\n\n### Q2: What is the key difference between ARG and ENV instructions?\n> **Answer:** `ARG` is only available during image build time and is not accessible inside running containers. `ENV` variables persist in both build and runtime.\n\n### Q3: Why is running a container as a non-root USER critical for production security?\n> **Answer:** If a container process running as root is compromised, an attacker breaking out of the container inherits root privileges on the host kernel. Running as an unprivileged user limits blast radius.\n\n### Q4: How do you copy a file from an earlier stage in a multi-stage Dockerfile?\n> **Answer:** Use `COPY --from=<stage_name_or_index> <source> <destination>`.\n",
    "mermaid": "graph LR\n    subgraph \"Stage 1: Build Environment (Heavy: 850 MB)\"\n        B1[\"Base: golang:alpine\"] --> B2[\"Source Code + Compiler\"]\n        B2 --> B3[\"Compile Binary: /app/myapp\"]\n    end\n    subgraph \"Stage 2: Production Runtime (Ultra-Light: 12 MB)\"\n        R1[\"Base: alpine:latest\"] --> R2[\"COPY --from=builder /app/myapp\"]\n        R2 --> R3[\"Minimal Attack Surface!\"]\n    end\n    B3 -.->|Only Binary Transferred| R2",
    "commands": "# DOCKER\n# --- STAGE 1: Builder ---\nFROM golang:1.20-alpine AS builder\n\n# Install build dependencies\nRUN apk update && apk add --no-cache git\n\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY go.mod go.sum* ./\nRUN go mod download\n\nCOPY . .\n# Statically compile binary with no external C dependencies\nRUN CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux go build -ldflags=\"-w -s\" -o /bin/api-server .\n\n# --- STAGE 2: Hardened Minimal Runtime ---\nFROM alpine:3.18\n\n# Create unprivileged security user\nRUN addgroup -S appgroup && adduser -S appuser -G appgroup\n\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=builder /bin/api-server /app/api-server\n\n# Switch to non-root user\nUSER appuser\n\nEXPOSE 8080\nENTRYPOINT [\"/app/api-server\"]",
