    "rawMarkdown": "# Day 13: Docker Compose - Multi-Container Orchestration & Full-Stack Deployment\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Course & Topic Overview\n- **Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)\n- **Module:** Multi-Container Stack Management & Declarative Infrastructure\n- **Key Concepts:** `compose.yaml` Syntax, Service Topologies, Custom Networks & Volumes, Startup Sequencing (`depends_on` + `healthcheck`), Stack Lifecycle\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why Docker Compose? (Declarative Orchestration)\n\nIn microservices architectures, manually managing dozens of `docker run` commands with volatile IP addresses, port bindings, and volume mounts leads to deployment fatigue and configuration drift. Docker Compose unifies the entire stack into a single version-controlled configuration file:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    ComposeFile[\"compose.yaml Configuration File\"] -->|docker compose up -d| Engine[Docker Engine]\n    subgraph \"Automated Stack Deployment\"\n        Engine --> Net[\"Custom Bridge Network: myapp_default\"]\n        Engine --> Vol[\"Persistent Volume: db_data\"]\n        Engine --> Web[\"Web Service (Nginx / WordPress)\"]\n        Engine --> API[\"API Service (Node.js / Python)\"]\n        Engine --> DB[\"Database Service (MySQL / Postgres)\"]\n        Web --- Net\n        API --- Net\n        DB --- Net\n        DB --- Vol\n    end\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complete Production `compose.yaml` Blueprint\n\n```yaml\nversion: \"3.8\"\n\nservices:\n  # Database Service\n  database:\n    image: postgres:15-alpine\n    container_name: prod_postgres\n    restart: unless-stopped\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_USER: appuser\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: SecurePassword123\n      POSTGRES_DB: main_production\n    volumes:\n      - pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data\n    networks:\n      - internal_network\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [\"CMD-SHELL\", \"pg_isready -U appuser -d main_production\"]\n      interval: 10s\n      timeout: 5s\n      retries: 5\n\n  # Application Backend Service\n  backend:\n    build:\n      context: ./backend\n      dockerfile: Dockerfile\n    container_name: prod_backend\n    restart: unless-stopped\n    environment:\n      DATABASE_URL: postgres://appuser:SecurePassword123@database:5432/main_production\n      PORT: 5000\n    depends_on:\n      database:\n        condition: service_healthy\n    networks:\n      - internal_network\n      - edge_network\n\n  # Web Reverse Proxy Frontend\n  frontend:\n    image: nginx:alpine\n    container_name: prod_proxy\n    restart: unless-stopped\n    ports:\n      - \"80:80\"\n      - \"443:443\"\n    volumes:\n      - ./nginx/nginx.conf:/etc/nginx/nginx.conf:ro\n    depends_on:\n      - backend\n    networks:\n      - edge_network\n\nvolumes:\n  pgdata:\n    driver: local\n\nnetworks:\n  edge_network:\n    driver: bridge\n  internal_network:\n    driver: bridge\n    internal: true\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Docker Compose CLI Masterclass\n\n| Command | Description | Production Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `docker compose up -d` | Builds images, creates networks/volumes, and launches all services in background. | Primary deployment command. |\n| `docker compose down` | Gracefully stops and removes containers and networks. | Leaves persistent volumes intact. |\n| `docker compose down -v` | Stops containers AND destroys all attached named volumes. | \u26a0\ufe0f **CAUTION:** Permanent data loss! |\n| `docker compose ps` | Displays the live status, health, and port mappings of all services. | Real-time health check. |\n| `docker compose logs -f [service]` | Follows real-time aggregated log output from all or specified containers. | Debugging & observability. |\n| `docker compose exec [service] sh` | Executes an interactive shell inside a running service container. | In-container troubleshooting. |\n| `docker compose restart [service]` | Restarts a specific container without affecting other stack services. | Safe rolling reload. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Critical Concept: `depends_on` vs Readiness Healthchecks\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> **Common Production Bug:** Setting `depends_on: ['db']` only waits until the DB container has STARTED (PID 1 spawned). It does NOT wait until MySQL/PostgreSQL has finished socket initialization and is ready to accept queries!\n> **Solution:** Always combine `depends_on` with `condition: service_healthy` and define a native container `healthcheck`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Interview & Real-World Troubleshooting (Q&A)\n\n### Q1: What is the difference between `docker-compose` and `docker compose`?\n> **Answer:** `docker-compose` (v1) was a standalone Python script; `docker compose` (v2) is a native, compiled Go plugin integrated directly into the Docker CLI with superior performance and feature parity.\n\n### Q2: How do containers defined in a `compose.yaml` discover and resolve each other?\n> **Answer:** By default, Compose creates a private user-defined bridge network named `<project_name>_default` and assigns each service an automatic DNS hostname equal to its service name.\n\n### Q3: Why does an application container crash with \"database connection refused\" even when `depends_on: [db]` is present?\n> **Answer:** Standard `depends_on` only checks container startup status. You must configure a `healthcheck` block on the database and specify `condition: service_healthy` under `depends_on`.\n\n### Q4: What does the `internal: true` network flag achieve in Docker Compose?\n> **Answer:** Setting `internal: true` on a bridge network disables external NAT gateway access, ensuring database containers cannot reach or be reached by the public internet.\n",
    "mermaid": "graph TD\n    ComposeFile[\"compose.yaml Configuration File\"] -->|docker compose up -d| Engine[Docker Engine]\n    subgraph \"Automated Stack Deployment\"\n        Engine --> Net[\"Custom Bridge Network: myapp_default\"]\n        Engine --> Vol[\"Persistent Volume: db_data\"]\n        Engine --> Web[\"Web Service (Nginx / WordPress)\"]\n        Engine --> API[\"API Service (Node.js / Python)\"]\n        Engine --> DB[\"Database Service (MySQL / Postgres)\"]\n        Web --- Net\n        API --- Net\n        DB --- Net\n        DB --- Vol\n    end",
