    "rawMarkdown": "# Day 14: Private Docker Registry - Self-Hosted Setup, TLS & Authentication\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udccc Course & Topic Overview\n- **Course:** Docker & Kubernetes (KR Network Cloud)\n- **Module:** Container Artifact Repositories & Enterprise Security\n- **Key Concepts:** Private Registry v2 Architecture, Plain HTTP vs TLS/SSL, `insecure-registries`, Basic Authentication (`htpasswd`), Image Push/Pull Workflow, HTTP Catalog API\n\n---\n\n## 1. Enterprise Use Case for Private Registries\n\nStoring proprietary production code on public registries (Docker Hub) introduces security, bandwidth, and compliance vulnerabilities. A self-hosted private registry provides total sovereignty over container artifacts:\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    subgraph \"CI/CD Pipeline & Devs\"\n        Dev([Developer Workstation])\n        CI([CI/CD Runner / Jenkins])\n    end\n    subgraph \"Corporate Data Center (Private LAN)\"\n        Reg[\"Private Registry v2<br/>(registry:2 on Port 5000)<br/>TLS + htpasswd Authentication\"]\n        Storage[(\"Persistent Storage<br/>/var/lib/registry\")]\n        Reg --- Storage\n    end\n    subgraph \"Production Cluster (Kubernetes / Docker Hosts)\"\n        Node1[\"Prod Worker Node 1\"]\n        Node2[\"Prod Worker Node 2\"]\n    end\n    Dev -->|docker push| Reg\n    CI -->|docker push| Reg\n    Reg -->|High-Speed 10Gbps LAN pull| Node1\n    Reg -->|High-Speed 10Gbps LAN pull| Node2\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Quickstart Deployment & The Insecure Registry Dilemma\n\nBy default, the Docker client enforces HTTPS encryption. Attempting to push to a plain HTTP registry fails with `http: server gave HTTP response to HTTPS client`:\n\n```bash\n# Launch basic registry on port 5000\ndocker run -d -p 5000:5000 --restart=always --name local-registry registry:2\n\n# Option A (Development Only): Configure Insecure Registry in /etc/docker/daemon.json\nsudo tee /etc/docker/daemon.json <<EOF\n{\n  \"insecure-registries\": [\"192.168.1.100:5000\"]\n}\nEOF\nsudo systemctl restart docker\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Production Hardening: TLS Certificates & Basic Auth\n\nEnterprise-grade registry configuration with TLS encryption and user authentication:\n\n```bash\n# Step 1: Generate self-signed TLS certificates\nmkdir -p /opt/registry/{certs,auth,data}\nopenssl req -newkey rsa:4096 -nodes -sha256 \\\n  -keyout /opt/registry/certs/domain.key \\\n  -x509 -days 365 \\\n  -out /opt/registry/certs/domain.crt \\\n  -subj \"/CN=registry.corp.internal\"\n\n# Step 2: Generate bcrypt htpasswd credentials\ndocker run --rm --entrypoint htpasswd httpd:2 -Bbn devuser SecureRegistryPass > /opt/registry/auth/htpasswd\n\n# Step 3: Run secure production-ready registry container\ndocker run -d \\\n  --name=enterprise-registry \\\n  --restart=always \\\n  -p 5000:5000 \\\n  -v /opt/registry/data:/var/lib/registry \\\n  -v /opt/registry/certs:/certs \\\n  -v /opt/registry/auth:/auth \\\n  -e REGISTRY_HTTP_TLS_CERTIFICATE=/certs/domain.crt \\\n  -e REGISTRY_HTTP_TLS_KEY=/certs/domain.key \\\n  -e \"REGISTRY_AUTH=htpasswd\" \\\n  -e \"REGISTRY_AUTH_HTPASSWD_REALM=Registry Realm\" \\\n  -e REGISTRY_AUTH_HTPASSWD_PATH=/auth/htpasswd \\\n  registry:2\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Push, Pull & Catalog Verification Workflow\n\n```bash\n# 1. Authenticate with private registry\ndocker login registry.corp.internal:5000 -u devuser -p SecureRegistryPass\n\n# 2. Tag local image with registry domain prefix\ndocker tag my-api:v1.0 registry.corp.internal:5000/backend/my-api:v1.0\n\n# 3. Push image into private repository\ndocker push registry.corp.internal:5000/backend/my-api:v1.0\n\n# 4. Pull image on production server\ndocker pull registry.corp.internal:5000/backend/my-api:v1.0\n\n# 5. Query Registry HTTP API v2 Catalog\ncurl -k -u devuser:SecureRegistryPass https://registry.corp.internal:5000/v2/_catalog\n# Output: {\"repositories\":[\"backend/my-api\"]}\n\n# Query tags of a specific repository\ncurl -k -u devuser:SecureRegistryPass https://registry.corp.internal:5000/v2/backend/my-api/tags/list\n# Output: {\"name\":\"backend/my-api\",\"tags\":[\"v1.0\"]}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Interview & Real-World Troubleshooting (Q&A)\n\n### Q1: Why does Docker reject pushing images to `http://<IP>:5000` by default?\n> **Answer:** The Docker daemon enforces HTTPS security for all external image registries to prevent man-in-the-middle tampering. If the target registry server speaks plain HTTP, Docker rejects the connection unless explicitly whitelisted in `/etc/docker/daemon.json` under `\"insecure-registries\"`.\n\n### Q2: How does Docker differentiate between an image destined for Docker Hub vs a Private Registry?\n> **Answer:** By the domain prefix in the image name. If the image name starts with a domain name or IP containing a dot or colon (`registry.example.com:5000/...`), Docker routes the push/pull request to that host. Otherwise, it defaults to `docker.io`.\n\n### Q3: How do you programmatically query the list of all images stored in a private Docker registry?\n> **Answer:** By using the native Registry HTTP API v2 endpoints: `GET /v2/_catalog` returns the JSON list of repositories, and `GET /v2/<repo>/tags/list` returns the list of available tags.\n\n### Q4: How do you ensure images stored in a private registry survive container recreation?\n> **Answer:** Mount a persistent host volume or network share to the container's storage path: `-v /mnt/nfs-storage:/var/lib/registry`, or configure an object storage driver (AWS S3, Azure Blob, Google Cloud Storage) in the registry's `config.yml`.\n",
